 types of solutions that end up being  heavily used by by one of our clients  like three four or five years down the  line and then no one's ever refactored  it so any code that anyone writes within  within gamma follows these six  principles it's versioned it's  reproducible on any on any any analysis  it's standardized using a standardized  project structure standardized Python  tooling it's well documented it's tested  and it follows all of BC G's data data  and code governance best practices  so with that we just like to conclude  with a picture of Matt looking down in a  marketing slide of we want a higher data  scientist we want to hire data engineers  and software engineers come join us  where we're growing fast  what's up we have a stand upstairs soon  so come by come get an umbrella come get  a pen it might be raining so an umbrella  is actually useful useful swag but  basically if you know Python or R and  are interested in working on some of the  world's largest analytics problems with  some of the world's largest companies  we'd love to have you questions  sure so most of it I would say most of  it is it's kind of like I mentioned that  earlier it's not necessarily like a like  a consultant client relationship right  where you come in wearing a suit  although I don't wear a suit but like  come in come in wearing a suit and and  talk to your you know like and be the  consultant that says like this is the  way things ought to happen or something  like that it's typically more like a  like a very much a collaboration what we  often see is that the reason why it's a  collaboration as well is because we are  together also building out the client's  capabilities to actually own the  products that we build for them so  typically what we see is that Sprint's  or other you know ways of working like  are fully integrated with the clients  and that like a lot of teams that we  have are not just only gamma it's also  the client in there it's also BCG  classic consul